# Run evidence is the record (soak-results provenance rule). The repo root
# ignores *.log and *.txt globally, which silently drops chaos.log, soak.log,
# streams-final.txt and friends from `git add runs/<id>` — an incomplete record
# that still looks committed. Re-include the small text evidence, but NOT
# logs, profiles, or dumps: those grow without bound, trip GitLab's 1 MiB
# blob cap, and their conclusions belong in manifest.md / stats.csv anyway.
!*.txt
!*.csv
!*.json
!*.out
# Logs stay local (the root *.log ignore applies here — no negation).
# Heap/CPU profiles captured by wedgewatch/probe: ~1-7 MiB each, and binary.
# Analyze locally (go tool pprof), record the conclusion in the manifest.
*.pb.gz
# Goroutine dumps: same story, multi-MiB text.
*.goroutines.txt
# Log tails captured by the stall watchdog. These are logs -- the root *.log
# ignore would have caught them -- but they are written as .log-tail.txt, and
# the `!*.txt` negation above re-admits them. One run's stall-capture is 13 MiB
# of them, individual files past GitLab's 1 MiB blob cap. Same rule as the rest:
# read them locally, put the conclusion in manifest.md.
*.log-tail.txt
node-logs.txt
node-logs-live.txt
container-logs/
reconcile-pulls.txt
# The convenience symlink to the newest run. Tracking it made soak.sh's own
# `ln -sfn` a tracked modification, so `git describe --dirty` reported -dirty
# for every run against an otherwise clean tree — which is precisely where the
# dirty marker needed to mean something (#4126).
latest

# A store copy streamed live from a node (capture-*/Shard*/) is raw data:
# tens of MB of segments, and crash-consistent at best. Its conclusions go in
# the capture README; the copy stays on the machine that took it.
Shard*/

# mem.csv is sampled every 30s for the length of a run: 1 MiB in twelve
# hours, past GitLab's blob cap, and its reading belongs in the manifest.
mem.csv
